.\" partyline(1) — man page. Rendered by `ptln man`; installed to man1 by the deb/rpm/cask.
.TH PARTYLINE 1 "2026-10-30" "partyline" "partyline manual"
.SH NAME
partyline, ptln \- session manager for AI coding agents, with repository memory and a chat between sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ptln
.br
.B ptln new
.I engine
.RI [ dir ]
.RB [ \-\-worktree
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-keep\-going
.IR N ]
.br
.B ptln memory
.I subcommand
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B ptln chat
.RI [ subcommand ]
.br
.B ptln
.I command
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
tmux 3.3 or newer is required to host sessions.
.PP
On one machine,
.B ptln
provides:
.TP
.B Session manager
Lists every Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, opencode, goose and Antigravity session on the machine,
read from each engine's own session files, across directories and git worktrees. Sessions open in
one terminal, hosted by a private tmux server, and keep running when the terminal closes.
.TP
.B Repository memory
Facts about a repository, one markdown file each in
.B memory/
inside that repository, committed with the code. They are rendered into
.BR AGENTS.md ,
.B CLAUDE.md
and
.B GEMINI.md
so every engine reads them when a session starts.
.TP
.B Chat
A room where you and your running sessions exchange messages, and the sessions answer each other.
.TP
.B Loops
Work a session builds in rounds: your checks run on it, then other models review it. See
.BR LOOPS .
.PP
There is no server and no account. partyline never commits to your repositories.
.PP
The binary is
.BR partyline ;
.B ptln
is a symlink to it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ptln
Open the session manager. With no sessions on the machine, open the first-run screen.
.TP
.B ptln new \fIengine\fR [\fIdir\fR] [\fIflags\fR]
Start a session in \fIdir\fR (default: the current directory). \fIengine\fR is one of claude,
codex, gemini, opencode, goose, antigravity. No arguments are passed to the engine: a word after
\fIengine\fR that is not a partyline flag is taken as \fIdir\fR, and anything after \fIdir\fR is
ignored.
.TP
.B ptln \-\-resume
Reopen the sessions that were open when you last quit, with the same models and permission modes.
.TP
.B ptln llms ls \fR[\-\-all]\fB \fR·\fB resume \fIid\fR \fB\fR·\fB prune \fR[\-\-apply]
List sessions newest first (\fB\-\-all\fR includes sessions started by other agents); resume one
session in the current terminal; list sessions whose directory or worktree no longer exists
(\fB\-\-apply\fR moves them to the trash).
.TP
.B ptln llms \-\-roots \fR·\fB \-\-look\-in \fIdir
Print the home directories sessions are read from; add another one (the \fBHOME\fR a session was
started under) to read sessions from.
.TP
.B ptln memory \fIsubcommand\fR
Read and write this repository's facts. See
.BR MEMORY .
.TP
.B ptln chat \fR[add|remove|who|send|read|listen]
Open the chat room, or manage it. See
.BR CHAT .
.TP
.B ptln loop \fIsubcommand\fR
Commands that agent sessions run, and \fBtab\fR, \fBstatus\fR, \fBresume\fR and \fBdelete\fR for you.
See
.BR LOOPS .
.TP
.B ptln tmux \fR[\-\-resume|\-\-quit|\-\-print\-conf]
The tmux server that hosts sessions. \fB\-\-quit\fR saves the set of open sessions, then stops
every session and the server; run it before updating an engine, then \fBptln \-\-resume\fR.
.TP
.B ptln wt \fR[ls|rm \fIbranch\fR|prune [\-\-yes]]
The git worktrees sessions were started in: list them, remove one, or remove those whose session
is gone (\fBprune\fR lists them; \fB\-\-yes\fR removes them).
.TP
.B ptln keepgoing \fR[status|off]
List the Claude sessions with keep-going armed, or disarm it on all of them.
.TP
.B ptln state
Print a JSON snapshot of the machine: live sessions, how many are waiting for input, and the
provider rate limit if one is in effect.
.TP
.B ptln theme \fR[Partyline|Blue|Mono|Paperwhite] [\-\-background auto|dark|light]
The colours partyline draws in. With no argument, print the palette, the background setting and,
for \fBauto\fR, what the terminal reported. \fBauto\fR asks the terminal its background colour
(OSC 11) when run outside tmux, and again each time partyline attaches to tmux unless the terminal has
never answered; the answer is saved for
the tab bar and popups. \fBt\fR in the session manager cycles the palettes.
.TP
.B ptln tray \fR[on|off]
macOS: start the menu-bar icon now and at login, or stop it and remove the login item. With no
argument, print whether it is on.
.TP
.B ptln upgrade
Update partyline the way it was installed: \fBbrew upgrade\fR for Homebrew, otherwise the install
script. \fBptln update\fR is an alias.
.TP
.B ptln version \fR·\fB ptln man \fR·\fB ptln help \fR·\fB ptln welcome
The version and whether a newer one exists; this page; the command list; the first-run screen.
.SH SESSION FLAGS
.TP
.B \-\-worktree \fIname\fR
Create a git worktree on a new branch \fIname\fR and start the session in it.
.TP
.B \-\-with\-state
With \fB\-\-worktree\fR, copy the current checkout's uncommitted changes and untracked files into
the new worktree.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-going \fIN\fR
Claude only. When the session stops, answer "continue?" for it, up to \fIN\fR times. The session
stops early if it prints the done token it is told about.
.TP
.B \-\-goal \fItext\fR
The instruction given with each keep-going continuation.
.SH SESSION MANAGER KEYS
.TP
.B arrows \fR·\fB g \fR/\fB G
Move; jump to the top or bottom.
.TP
.B / \fR·\fB s \fR·\fB R
Search; cycle the sort order (last used, oldest, project); refresh.
.TP
.B ⏎
Open the highlighted session. A permission-mode picker comes first.
.TP
.B space
Select several sessions; \fB⏎\fR opens them in one terminal.
.TP
.B o
Open the session in a new terminal tab or window.
.TP
.B N \fR·\fB ctrl\-n \fR·\fB n
New session now; the same with permissions bypassed; new session with options.
.TP
.B |
Pick two sessions and show them side by side.
.TP
.B p \fR·\fB r \fR·\fB d
Pin; rename; show \fBgit diff\fR for the session's repository.
.TP
.B x
Delete. The confirmation shows the session's age, branch, worktree, uncommitted files and whether
other sessions are live in the same tree. A running session cannot be deleted. Files move to
\fB~/.partyline/trash/\fR.
.TP
.B a \fR·\fB t \fR·\fB D \fR·\fB ? \fR·\fB q
Show or hide agent sessions; colour theme; open the documentation; list every key; quit.
.SH IN A SESSION
Press \fBctrl\-\\\fR to open the menu, then a key. \fB←\fR/\fB→\fR or \fB1\fR\-\fB9\fR switch
sessions; esc or \fBctrl\-\\\fR again closes it.
.TP
.B n \fR·\fB N \fR·\fB t
New session with options; new session, asking for the engine and permissions; shell window.
.TP
.B o
The session manager.
.TP
.B r
On Sessions: rename the highlighted session, as \fBr\fR in the session manager does; an empty
name restores the default. On Commands: the relay settings (deploy or update, the saved
Cloudflare token).
.TP
.B c
The chat room as a popup; \fBctrl\-t\fR in it pins it as a tab.
.TP
.B l
The Loops popup (\fBptln loop tab\fR): every loop and batch on the machine, in five columns
\(em what needs you, then planned, in progress, in review, done. \fB⏎\fR shows a row's rounds and
findings, or selects the contractor of a building or reviewing card on the Contractors tab; \fBa\fR on a row that needs you
opens the approval popup. A done row whose session recorded its PR with
\fBptln loop pr <id> <url-or-number>\fR shows \fB#\fR\fIn\fR and the PR's checks, which the watcher
asks \fBgh pr checks\fR for at most every 2 minutes. A batch's steps are built by builders
partyline starts, which it commits when they pass; nothing is pushed.
A round or batch review whose reviewer could not run (a usage limit, a crash) never passes on the
other reviewers' word: the run shows \fB⏸ paused\fR in review, its note names the reviewer and the
error, and it resumes by itself when a limit lifts, or with \fBptln loop resume <id>\fR.
\fBd\fR on a card, then \fBd\fR again, stops the loop and deletes it for good (a batch step deletes
its whole batch), as \fBptln loop delete <id>\fR does. \fBr\fR shows one repository at a time, then
all again; \fB,\fR opens the loop settings.
The mouse works too: click a card or contractor to select it, click the selected one to open it, click Board or Contractors to switch, and the wheel moves between cards or scrolls the details.
.TP
.B ,
Loop settings: who builds new batches (an engine, model and effort, the first choice then
backups), who reviews them (slots, each a list to fail over through), whether to fail over on
usage limits, the quality standard (built in, or a file), Decisions (\fBloops decide\fR, the
default, or \fBask me\fR), the arbiter for disputes (with \fBloops decide\fR, reviewer 1 unless you
choose one), and an optional Jev key.
With \fBask me\fR, Disputes chooses whether disputes come to you or go to the arbiter. Saved in
\fI~/.partyline/settings.json\fR. A loop or batch started without \fB--config\fR or \fB--review\fR copies
them into its config, and keeps the settings it started with. See
.BR LOOPS .
.TP
.B k
Open Loops on its Contractors tab with the first contractor selected. Clicking a chip opens
Loops with that contractor selected. The contractor row appears above the tabs
while a contractor runs or a finished one (\fB✓\fR or \fB✗\fR) has not been seen. \fB⚙N\fR on a
tab counts the contractors that session's loops have running.
.TP
.B a
The approval popup. With Decisions set to \fBask me\fR, loops wait here for you: a popup with what
each one will do. With the default, \fBloops decide\fR, nothing waits. Approving asks you to type
a four-digit code the popup shows in large digits: agents can type into a popup with
\fBtmux send-keys -K\fR but cannot read it. As you type, the digits typed turn green and a blinking cursor marks the next one. After a decision the popup says what it did and closes after 3 seconds (esc closes it now), or goes back to the list when more is waiting. \fB‼\fR on a tab and "N to approve" on the status bar show when one is waiting.
A batch of loops is approved once, after the reviewers have read its briefs as a set; its
steps then run one after another without stopping. With \fBask me\fR, a finding disputed during a
step is deferred to you (or ruled on by the arbiter, when Disputes says so).
\fBptln loop batch status\fR lists what each step did.
.TP
.B w \fR·\fB g
Fork this session into a git worktree; arm or disarm keep-going.
.TP
.B [ \fR·\fB | \fR(or\fB +\fR)\fB \fR·\fB \- \fR·\fB \\\\
Scroll history; bring the highlighted session in beside this one (\fB+\fR does the same); move it
back to its own tab; open a shell pane beside this one.
.TP
.B x \fR·\fB q \fR·\fB Q
Close the highlighted session; detach, leaving everything running; quit every session (saved first).
.SH MEMORY
Each fact is a markdown file with YAML front matter in \fB<repo>/memory/\fR. One file per fact
means facts added on two branches merge without conflict.
.TP
.B ptln memory build \fR[\-\-apply] [\-\-remove\-stale]
Set up or refresh a repository. With \fB\-\-apply\fR it writes a description of the project
(kind \fBoverview\fR), generated by claude with read-only tools from the last commit, when the
repository has none; build, test and CI facts it can cite from the files; and, on first setup,
open questions that only a person can answer. Without \fB\-\-apply\fR it prints what it would write. It never changes a fact a
person wrote. A derived fact whose evidence is gone is listed, and removed only with
\fB\-\-remove\-stale\fR.
.TP
.B ptln memory build \-\-apply \-\-refresh
Rewrite the overview from the last commit, starting from the current one, in place. \fBbuild\fR
also reports how many code commits have landed since the overview was last committed, and lists
facts that name a file changed since the fact was last committed.
.TP
.B ptln memory verify \fIid\fR...
Mark facts as checked against the code at HEAD by writing \fBverified:\fR into each. A fact counts
as checked at the last commit that changed its file, so committing the verify clears the flag.
.TP
.B ptln memory add \fIkind\fR "\fItext\fR"
Record a fact. Kinds: \fBoverview\fR (one per repository), \fBdecision\fR, \fBconstraint\fR,
\fBcontract\fR, \fBgotcha\fR, \fBquestion\fR. An unknown kind is refused with the kind probably
meant.
.TP
.B ptln memory ls \fR[\-\-all]\fB \fR·\fB brief \fR·\fB where
List current facts; print what a session is told at start; print where the facts are and how many.
.TP
.B ptln memory edit \fIid\fR "\fItext\fR" \fR·\fB append \fIid\fR "\fItext\fR" \fR·\fB rm \fIid
Rewrite, extend or delete a fact. An id prefix is enough. \fBgit log \-p memory/\fR is the history.
.TP
.B ptln memory map
Rewrite the generated block in \fBAGENTS.md\fR, \fBCLAUDE.md\fR and \fBGEMINI.md\fR. Every fact
change already does this.
.TP
.B ptln memory hooks \fR[\-\-install]
Report which engines refresh facts during a session and which still need a trust grant;
\fB\-\-install\fR adds the hook where that can be done safely.
.TP
.B ptln memory harvest \fR[\-n] [\-\-since \fIrev\fR]
Record facts that commits declare in \fBPtln\-Decision:\fR, \fBPtln\-Constraint:\fR,
\fBPtln\-Contract:\fR, \fBPtln\-Gotcha:\fR or \fBPtln\-Question:\fR trailers. \fB\-n\fR prints
them without writing.
.TP
.B ptln memory propose \-\-from \fIsource\fR \fIkind\fR "\fItext\fR"
Record a fact taken from another system. At least one \fB\-\-from\fR citation
(\fIsystem\fR:\fIid\fR) is required.
.SS Rules
A fact is refused, with the reason, if it records the state of a machine, the status of a work
item, or something true only for the current session. The refusal quotes the words that matched.
Questions are exempt. An overview is not checked this way, on add, edit or append.
.PP
partyline writes facts and leaves them uncommitted. It creates \fBAGENTS.md\fR only when the
repository has none of the three files, and changes only the block between its markers.
.SS Setup from a session
The first time a partyline session runs in a git repository with no \fBmemory/\fR, the agent
finishes your request, then offers once to set memory up. If you agree, it runs
\fBptln memory build \-\-apply\fR, checks the overview, answers the open questions it can verify,
drafts answers to the rest for you to correct, and records a small number of facts, each naming its
evidence. It does not commit. The offer is made once per repository on
this machine.
.PP
When the code is 20 or more commits past the overview, the first turn of a session tells the agent
to ask you once whether to update the memory; if you decline, it asks again after another 20.
.SS During a session
Claude sessions started or resumed by partyline get two hooks: one shows facts recorded since the
session's last turn, and one records up to two facts from the session's recent work, at most once
every 20 minutes, in repositories that already have \fBmemory/\fR.
.SH CHAT
.TP
.B ptln chat
Open the room (from a session: \fBctrl\-\\\fR then \fBc\fR opens it as a popup). Type to post; a message goes to everyone unless it contains \fB@\fR\fItab\fR (for
example \fB@11\fR) or \fB@\fR\fIengine\fR. Messages are rendered as Markdown. In the room:
\fB/add\fR \fItab\fR... adds sessions by tab number (\fB11.2\fR for the second pane of a split
tab), \fB/remove\fR \fItab\fR, \fB/who\fR, \fB/stop\fR [\fItab\fR] and \fB/go\fR [\fItab\fR]
pause and resume delivery, \fB/clear\fR, \fB/help\fR lists every key, \fBctrl\-c\fR leaves and
keeps an unsent draft. Above the input, one line per session shows whether your message is
waiting, being worked on, or answered.
.IP
Editing: \fBShift\-Enter\fR, \fBAlt\-Enter\fR or \fB\\\fR then Enter add a line break; arrows,
Home/End, \fBctrl\-a\fR/\fBctrl\-e\fR and Option-arrows move; \fB↑\fR/\fB↓\fR move between lines,
then through what you sent; Option-Backspace or \fBctrl\-w\fR deletes a word; \fBctrl\-u\fR and
\fBctrl\-k\fR delete to the start and end of the line; \fBctrl\-z\fR undoes; \fBTab\fR completes
commands, tab numbers and mentions; \fBctrl\-r\fR searches sent messages; \fBctrl\-g\fR opens
\fB$EDITOR\fR; Esc twice clears the input.
While going through what you sent, Esc returns to your draft. \fBCtrl\-Enter\fR also adds a line break; \fBctrl\-d\fR deletes forward, and on an empty
line leaves the room. \fB/rm\fR is short for \fB/remove\fR. \fBctrl\-t\fR pins the room as a tab
(and, in the tab, closes it).
.TP
.B ptln chat add \fItab\fR... \fR·\fB remove \fItab\fR... \fR·\fB who
Manage members without opening the room.
.TP
.B ptln chat send "\fItext\fR" \fR·\fB read \fR·\fB listen
What sessions run: post a reply; print waiting messages; wait for a message, print it and exit.
.SS Delivery
A Claude session that is working receives waiting messages from its Stop hook when its turn ends.
An idle Claude session keeps \fBptln chat listen\fR running as a background task; it exits when a
message arrives, and Claude Code then gives the session a turn. Sessions reply with
\fBptln chat send\fR.
.PP
A session with nothing listening (Codex, or a session started before its hooks were installed) is
sent one line pointing it at \fBptln chat read\fR. The room types that line only when the pane is
not shown on an attached terminal, the session is not working, and its input box is empty; it
then checks that the line was submitted. Otherwise the message waits.
.PP
After 16 consecutive messages from sessions, delivery to sessions stops until you post.
.SH LOOPS
Tell a Claude, Codex or Gemini session running in partyline "use a partyline loop for this".
Its prompt hook or managed repository memory block points to
.B ptln loop guide
for the calling engine's workflow: briefs, checks, approval and review results. A Codex or Gemini
session with neither a trusted hook nor a managed memory block gets no pointer.
.PP
A single run (\fBptln loop start\fR) is built by the session. A batch (\fBptln loop batch start\fR)
is two or more ordered steps, built by contractors partyline starts; partyline commits each step
that passes in the batch's checkout and never pushes. Gemini can start batches, not single runs.
Each round runs the brief's checks on the work, then, when no check newly fails, the reviewers
review it.
.SS Decisions
By default loops decide for themselves and nothing waits for you. A brief or batch waiting for
approval is approved. A disputed finding is ruled on by a model that did not build the
work (the arbiter, else reviewer 1); its ruling stands, and with no valid answer the finding
stands. With a Jev key saved in the loop settings, the brief (up to 6000 characters), the finding
and the dispute are sent to Jev (api.typesafe.ai) and its estimate goes into that model's prompt;
without a key, or when Jev fails, the dispute goes on without it.
.PP
With Decisions set to \fBask me\fR in the loop settings (\fBctrl\-\\\fR then \fB,\fR), you approve
briefs and rule on disputes in the approval popup (\fBctrl\-\\\fR then \fBa\fR). The session must
never approve.
.PP
Before the first loop, choose the builder and reviewers (engine, model and effort) in the loop
settings. A loop started before then is saved and paused, the settings open on your screen, and
it carries on once they are chosen.
.SS Configuration
\fBptln loop start\fR and \fBptln loop batch start\fR accept \fB\-\-config <file>\fR. The JSON
contains \fBreviewers\fR, an array of slots, each an ordered array of
\fIengine\fR:\fImodel\fR[:\fIeffort\fR] strings (engine claude or codex); \fBfailover\fR (default
true); and, for a batch, \fBbuilder\fR, one such string or a list tried in order (default: the
engine that started the batch, else claude). The batch document and approval cover this config;
the popup shows the order and failover setting. Without \fB\-\-config\fR,
\fB\-\-review claude,codex\fR names one reviewer per slot; with neither, the run or batch copies the
loop settings into its config. Known usage, rate and capacity limits are recorded per model in
\fI~/.partyline/limits.json\fR; a limited model is skipped until its reset (12 hours if unknown).
A slot tries its next model; if every slot is limited, the review waits until the earliest reset.
With failover false, a limit fails that review. \fBptln loop batch status\fR lists the model used
for each step and review round.
.SS Commands for you
.TP
.B ptln loop tab \fR[\-\-contractor \fIid\fR]
The Loops board full-screen (the popup is \fBctrl\-\\\fR then \fBl\fR); with \fB\-\-contractor\fR, its
Contractors tab with that contractor selected.
.TP
.B ptln loop status \fR[\fIid\fR]
Print the state of every loop, or of one.
.TP
.B ptln loop resume \fIid
Run a paused review again. A review paused by a usage limit resumes by itself.
.TP
.B ptln loop delete \fIid
Stop a loop and remove it for good, so it cannot be resumed; a batch step deletes its whole batch.
\fBd\fR twice on its card does the same.
.TP
.B ptln loop review \fR[\-\-by claude,codex[:\fImodel\fR]] [\-\-base \fIref\fR]
A cross-model review of the working tree against \fIref\fR (default: where the branch left main),
with no run. Exit 0 approve, 1 a P1 finding, 2 a reviewer failed.
.SS Loop inbox
.B ptln loop inbox <id>
prints the run state and waiting messages, then exits. Claude uses loop listen in the background; Codex receives a guarded idle wake; Gemini batch results arrive with its next prompt.
.SH MOBILE REMOTE
.TP
.B ptln relay deploy [--domain <host>]
Deploy the embedded Cloudflare Worker and SQLite Durable Object in your account.
Uses workers.dev by default, or a custom domain you own. Reads CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
for one run (or prompts without echo); never saves it. Requires Node.js and npm.
Reports saved Wrangler credentials. Records URL, host as RP ID and machine id in
~/.partyline/remote/relay.json (0600); the machine token is in relay-token (0600).
.TP
.B ptln relay invite
Print a one-use invite valid for ten minutes. Requires a registered machine.
.TP
.B ptln relay join <string>
Redeem an invite and register this machine; refuses existing local configuration.
.TP
.B ptln relay status
Show URL, RP ID and whether this machine's watcher is connected. Unknown connectivity
is an error. The watcher connects outward while the Partyline tmux server is running.

.SS Mobile pairing
.B ptln remote pair
Opens a private tmux popup with a QR code and a typed pairing string valid for ten minutes.
Paste into the relay app. The separate one-use device invite admits only a pairing route;
the Noise PSK never goes to the relay. Create a passkey on the phone, compare the eight-group
phone fingerprint on both screens, then approve with the code in the popup.
No pairing is saved and no device token is issued before approval. Keep the popup open.
.TP
.B ptln remote phones
List paired phone ids, names, fingerprints, pairing times and last seen. Pairings are saved
in ~/.partyline/remote/phones.json and changes in events.jsonl, both mode 0600.
.TP
.B ptln remote revoke <id>
Remove a phone and revoke its relay device token. The approval popup also lists phones;
select a phone and confirm its revocation there. Closing live sessions is a later step.
.SH ENGINES
Facts reach every engine through \fBAGENTS.md\fR, \fBCLAUDE.md\fR or \fBGEMINI.md\fR at session
start, with no hook. Refreshing during a session needs a hook:
.TP
.B Claude
\fBUserPromptSubmit\fR and \fBStop\fR hooks, passed per session at launch. No configuration file
and no trust prompt.
.TP
.B Codex
\fBUserPromptSubmit\fR. Trust is granted once in the Codex TUI. An untrusted hook is disabled
without a warning, so its state is reported as unknown.
.TP
.B Gemini
\fBBeforeAgent\fR. Needs the workspace trusted, or \fB\-\-skip\-trust\fR.
.TP
.B Antigravity
\fBPreInvocation\fR. Needs the directory in \fBtrustedWorkspaces\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B <repo>/memory/*.md
The repository's facts.
.TP
.B <repo>/AGENTS.md\fR, \fBCLAUDE.md\fR, \fBGEMINI.md
The generated block between \fB<!\-\- partyline:memory BEGIN\fR and \fBEND \-\->\fR markers.
.TP
.B ~/.partyline/
Session index, saved workspace (\fBllms\-workspace.json\fR), generated tmux configuration,
delete trash, the chat room (\fBchat/\fR), per-repository markers for the memory setup offer, the
chosen palette (\fBllms\-theme\fR) and background setting (\fBtheme\-background\fR), loop runs
and batches (\fBloops/\fR), contractor records (\fBcontractors/\fR), loop settings
(\fBsettings.json\fR), model usage limits (\fBlimits.json\fR), the Jev key (\fBjev\-key\fR,
mode 0600), the latest release seen (\fBversion\-check.json\fR), the update popup's choices
(\fBupdates.json\fR) and automatic updates' output (\fBupdate.log\fR).
.SH COMPLETE KEY REFERENCE
M-1 through M-9 (Alt-1 through Alt-9) select a window directly.
PgUp / PgDn page the launcher list.

.SS tmux

.TP
.B (none)
prefix None: no tmux prefix

.TP
.B C-\e
run-shell 'ptln tmux --menu', the menu popup

.TP
.B M-1 … M-9
select-window -t N

.TP
.B WheelUpPane
full-screen apps keep the wheel; otherwise copy-mode -e; send -M

